In Kotlin, curly braces are interpreted according to the grammar position:

- A **code block** is allowed where a construct expects a `controlStructureBody`, such as the body of `if`, `else`, `for`, `while`, `do-while`, or a `when` entry. The grammar defines this as either a `block` or a single `statement` ([Kotlin grammar](https://kotlinlang.org/grammar/)).
- Kotlin does **not** support arbitrary standalone code blocks as statements. A `{ ... }` in an ordinary statement position is interpreted as a **lambda literal** ([Kotlin specification](https://kotlinlang.org/spec/statements.html)).
- Expressions can also be used as statements, so a lambda may appear as a statement, although it may have no useful effect ([Kotlin expressions specification](https://github.com/Kotlin/kotlin-spec/blob/release/docs/src/md/kotlin.core/expressions.md)).

For example:

```kotlin
if (condition) {
    println("yes")
}
```

Here the braces are the `if` body.

But this:

```kotlin
val f = {
    println("called")
}
```

defines a lambda.

Kotlin has no general-purpose block expression like some other languages. To evaluate several statements and obtain the last expression’s value, use a function such as `run`:

```kotlin
val result = run {
    val a = 1
    val b = 2
    a + b
}
```

A block’s value is its last expression; if it has no last expression, its value is `Unit` ([Kotlin specification](https://kotlinlang.org/spec/statements.html)).

Also, braces following `if`, `else`, `for`, `while`, or `when` are taken as control-structure bodies. If a lambda is required in such a position, an additional pair of braces may be needed:

```kotlin
val a: () -> Int = if (condition) {
    { 1 }
} else {
    { 2 }
}
```

The outer braces are the `if` branches; the inner braces are the lambdas.
